      "files": ["src/app.js"],
      "evidence": "Global CORS middleware `app.use(cors({ origin: true, credentials: true }))` is applied before `app.use('/api', apiRouter)`. `origin: true` reflects any request `Origin` header into the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` response header (every origin is whitelisted), while `credentials: true` sets `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. Combined, the browser attaches the victim's session cookie/HTTP-auth to any cross-origin `fetch('https://<host>/api/...', { credentials: 'include' })` and, because the server echoes the attacker's origin, allows the attacker's JavaScript to read the authenticated response. This exposes every session-bearing endpoint under `/api` to arbitrary origins (credential theft / cross-origin data read). This is distinct from src/auth.js (`jwt.verify(token, JWT_SECRET, { algorithms: ['HS256'] })`), which pins the algorithm and is not exploitable (alg:none and RS256->HS256 confusion are blocked).",
      "remediation": "Do not combine origin reflection/wildcard with credentials. Replace `origin: true` with an explicit allowlist, e.g. `cors({ origin: ['https://app.example.com'], credentials: true })`, validating against the request Origin. For endpoints that do not need cookies, set `credentials: false`. Apply CORS per-router/per-route with the minimum origin set rather than globally to the whole `/api` surface."
    }
  ],
  "scanned_files": ["src/app.js", "src/auth.js"],
  "source_modified": false
}
"}

